          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The strongest shared consensus identifies symbolic “day” with light, moral clarity, righteous or divinely accountable activity in contrast to night and darkness. It also recognizes context-specific day-for-year and day-as-thousand-years interpretations, while denying that either is a universal conversion rule.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book identifies day as the time or sphere of light, exposure, and accountable action, opposed to night, and conditionally allows year-day or thousand-year scaling in prophetic contexts.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Day signifies light and the sphere of revealed, righteous, wakeful action, contrasted with night or darkness.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Day is the time of light, exposure, and accountable action, contrasted with night.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement appears. The book’s conditional phrase “may also scale” is compatible with the consensus that year-day and thousand-year readings are context-dependent rather than universal. The book simply omits other established uses such as the Day of the Lord.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book neither replaces the consensus referent nor imposes an unconditional time-conversion formula.\",\"rationale\":\"The minimum core identifications are equivalent: both associate day with light and responsible godly activity over against night and darkness. The added references to a year or a thousand years are already represented among the consensus alternatives and are qualified by context, so they do not create a substantive departure.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Numbers 14:34 and Ezekiel 4:6 directly support a day-for-year correspondence in their particular enacted judgments, but 2 Peter 3:8 expresses God’s transcendence over human time and does not by itself establish a prophetic counting rule equating one day with one thousand years.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
